{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790966792,"nonce":"xLPImITKwBL3/Vi7tNHNpo6XrxY=","title":"FSM identity is (module, name); the compiler hardcoded \\\"main\\\" and the runtime registry","message":"Two files each declaring a `Ticker` shared one definition, and the\nsecond to compile replaced the first. Its transitions went with it, so\nthe first file's triggers matched nothing and did nothing.\n\nTwo causes, stacked:\n\n- `compile_source_with_namespace` derives this file's module and threads\n  it through every pass, then handed the FSM registry the literal\n  `\"main\"`. `FsmId { module, type_id }` collapsed to one module for\n  every program in the process.\n- `blinc_runtime`'s registry keyed `name_index` on the bare name, and\n  `register` treats a repeat as a replacement -- right for recompiling a\n  file, wrong for a second file.\n\nNow: the module is part of `FsmDefinition` and of the key, replacement\nis per module, `id_in(module, name)` is precise, and `id_of(name)`\nanswers only when one module declares it. `find_visible` handles the\nimported case, where the mention has already been rewritten to the\nexporter's mangled name.\n\n**Signals were always right** -- `signal::lookup` resolves per module and\nreports `Ambiguous`. FSMs were the outlier. Two test programs sharing a\nsignal name is therefore an unwritable name, not a bug: give each\nprogram its own.\n\n**Test hygiene this forces:** the machine driver, both registries and the\nreactive graph are process-global while a `BlincDsl` is not. A test file\nwhere several tests each build a DSL needs a `static LOCK` (see\n`multi_module.rs`, `with_blocks.rs`), or they compile into each other's\nstate. Related: project fsm deferred handler capture.","files":null}]}